BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:2026 Toronto Book Awards Prize Ceremony
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261110T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261110T200000
UID:6a85b338bf1aec09082b9f2b@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:The winner of the 2026 Toronto Book Awards will be announced a
 t the annual event at the Appel Salon inside the Toronto Reference Library
 . Author and comedian\nAli Hassan\nwill be joined by this year's finalists
 \, and other notable Torontonians to celebrate the City and its literary a
 ccomplishments.\nThe City of Toronto and Toronto Public Library will annou
 nce the winner of the 2026 Toronto Book Awards shortlisted titles.\nThis y
 ear's titles reflect the creativity and diversity of Toronto's literary co
 mmunity\, and these stories that are inspired by the City will leave a las
 ting impact on readers.\nThe titles selected for the 2026 shortlist are:\n
 Cherry Beach\nby Don Gillmore\nGood Guys\nby Sharon Bala\nProperty\nby Kat
 e Cayley\nYou Started It\nby Jackie Khalilieh\nThe rest of the titles sel
 ected for the 2026 longlist are:\nBlack Public Joy\nby Jay Pitter\nBook of
  Lives\nby Margaret Atwood\nFatal Confession\nby Carolyn Strange\nHousing 
 for All\nby Frank Lewinburg\nJuniper\nby Kerri Huffman\nThe Paper Birds\nb
 y Jeanette Lynes\nPrecarious\nby Marcello Di Cintio\nTyson's Song\nby Pete
 r Bailey\nWhere Histories Meet\nby Victoria Freeman\nBooks available for p
 urchase. Book signing to follow.\n***\nEstablished by Toronto City Council
  in 1974\, the Toronto Book Awards honour books of literary merit that are
  evocative of Toronto.\nThis year's Toronto Book Awards Committee is compr
 ised of volunteer members Jake Byrne\, Kristen den Hartog\, Julia Kim\, M
 atthew R. Morris\, and Banoo Zan.\nThis year\, the City is doubling the pr
 ize amounts: the winning author will receive $20\,000\, and each shortlist
 ed finalist will receive $2\,000.\nLearn more about the Toronto Book Award
 s and the finalists:\nCity of Toronto\nToronto Public Library\n***\nPLAN Y
 OUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto Reference Libr
 ary (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor\nTime:\nDoors open 6:00pm\, Event start
 s 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registration required.\nTicket policy:\nWe overs
 ell these events to ensure that the greatest number of people have an oppo
 rtunity to attend. Tickets guaranteed until 6:45pm or until capacity is re
 ached. Seating first come\, first served.\nBar\n: Beer/wine available for 
 purchase (debit/credit only).\nAccessibility:\nTPL is committed to accessi
 bility. If you are Deaf or have a disability and would like to request acc
 ommodation for this event\, please contact accessibleservices@tpl.ca or 41
 6-393-7099 at least three weeks prior to the event.\n***\nThis event is pa
 rt of our signature\nSalon Series\n\, where we host local and internationa
 l authors\, artists and thinkers in conversation about their new books and
  big ideas.\n***\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information
  on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public 
 Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will o
 nly be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. 
 Registrants may be contacted to fill out a brief survey following the prog
 ram. Questions about how the library handles your information can be direc
 ted to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge St
 reet\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Appel Salon\, Second Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b338bf1aec09082b9f2b
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:NatureTO: Trees Around the Branch Tour
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261005T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261005T193000
UID:6a495489541102b7fa8a674a@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:The Riverdale branch and its neighbourhood is home to a vari
 ety of trees. Join the City of Toronto Urban Forestry staff to explore the
  natural world on a local neighbourhood tour. Learn how to recognize diffe
 rent species and fun facts about their cultural and ecological importance.
 \nChildren under 12 must be accompanied by a parent/caregiver.\nThe tour w
 ill start and end at the Riverdale Branch and will be guided by the City 
 of Toronto Urban Forestry staff. There may be uneven\, unpaved surfaces al
 ong the route. Please dress and prepare accordingly for this outdoor progr
 am (e.g. gloves/mittens\, hats\, sunscreen\, water\, comfortable shoes). T
 his program takes place rain or shine. In the event of severe inclement we
 ather\, registrants will be notified of an alternative date.\nThis program
  is part of TPL's\nOur Fragile Planet\nprogram series\, supported by Frien
 ds of Toronto Public Library\, South Chapter.​\nInformation Collection N
 otice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto 
 Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, a
 nd 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program registra
 tion\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles yo
 ur information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\
 n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Riverdale\, Room 1
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a495489541102b7fa8a674a
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
