BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Surviving Dementia Care: The Realities of Family Caregiving
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260928T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260928T193000
UID:6a7224bbb46787079ead9120@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Are you a caregiver for someone with dementia? This talk add
 resses the specific experiences of people who are caring for a loved one\
 , including the points of stress\, the points of joy\, and ways in which 
 caregivers learn to survive the experience.\nLearn about understanding the
  diagnosis\, communicating with the loved one\, managing guilt and grief\
 , choosing an assisted living facility\, using music and the arts as a me
 ans of connection and how the loving bond within the family can be maintai
 ned for as long as possible.\nPresented by D. Grant Campbell\, author of\n
 Surviving Dementia Care\n(University of Toronto Press\, 2026).\nD. Grant C
 ampbell is a Professor Emeritus of Library and Information Science in the 
 Faculty of Information and Media Studies\, at the University of Western On
 tario in London\, Ontario. He holds a PhD in English Literature from Queen
 ’s University and a Master of Information Studies from the University of
  Toronto. A violinist for over 50 years\, he has played in numerous commun
 ity orchestras\, and has also conducted music sessions in various dementia
  settings in Toronto and London.\n​\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe
  personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Libr
 ary under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). T
 he information will only be used for event and program registration\, deli
 very and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your informat
 ion can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-
 5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Gerrard/Ashdale\, 2nd Floor Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a7224bbb46787079ead9120
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Bridging Minds: Menopause and Brain Health – Memory\, Thinking a
 nd Dementia Risk
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260916T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260916T190000
UID:6a86058c3b6c71003e59f77c@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Menopause can affect many aspects of health\, including how we
  think and feel. But what do we actually know about its effects on memory 
 and thinking\, and is there a connection between menopause and dementia la
 ter in life?\nIn the first of two Bridging Minds webinars to focus on meno
 pause\, join Alexis Reeves\, Scientist at Women’s College Hospital\, to 
 explore what current research tells us about menopause and brain health. L
 earn about changes in memory and thinking that can happen during menopause
 \, whether they continue afterward\, and what researchers are learning abo
 ut factors that may influence brain health and dementia risk later in life
 .\nWhat to Expect\nMenopause and your health:\nSetting the stage for what 
 we know about this immensely important life transition\, and it’s impact
 s of longer-term health\nMemory and thinking during and after menopause:\n
 Do symptoms related to memory\, concentration or thinking “bounce back
 ” after menopause?\nMenopause and brain health later in life:\nExplore w
 hat researchers know about how factors such as the age at perimenopause\, 
 the type and severity of symptoms\, and menopausal hormone therapy may be 
 related to brain health and dementia risk later in life\nBio:\nAlexis Reev
 es is an epidemiologist\, scientist at Women’s College Hospital Research
  and Innovation Institute and assistant professor at University of Toronto
 . Her research focuses on the causes and consequences of disparities in ac
 celerated aging.  Much of her work has focused on the health of women\, es
 pecially Black women\, as they enter into life-stages\, such as the midlif
 e menopausal transition where cardio-metabolic risk is high and disparitie
 s can exacerbate.  Overall\, her work aims to advance health equity and su
 pport minority populations to thrive as they age.\nAbout the Series\nBridg
 ing Minds: Health & Science Conversations is a partnership between the Tor
 onto Public Library and Women’s College Hospital. Through monthly discus
 sions led by experts from across different clinical areas and scientific d
 isciplines\, this series provides attendees with the opportunity to engage
  directly with our topics and speakers and empowers communities to navigat
 e health and science topics confidently\, inspiring a future of informed d
 ecision-making.\nLearn more:\nhttps://www.womenscollegehospital.ca/bridgin
 g-minds/\nReserve Your Spot Today!\nThis virtual Zoom event is free to joi
 n but registration is required. Once registered\, you’ll receive an emai
 l with the Zoom link.\nWe can’t wait to see you there!
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a86058c3b6c71003e59f77c
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
