BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Crossing the Wine-Dark Sea: Emily Wilson on Translating Antiquity
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260929T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260929T200000
UID:6a85b5d0bf1aec09082b9f55@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Renowned classicist and translator Emily Wilson explores the a
 rt and challenges of bringing ancient literature to modern readers.\nIn Cr
 ossing the Wine-Dark Sea\, Emily Wilson — whose acclaimed renditions of 
 Homer's Odyssey and Iliad have captured a generation's imagination — tak
 es readers on a sharp and often entertaining tour through antiquity. Drawi
 ng on twenty years of scholarship and teaching\, Wilson illuminates the di
 fficult choices translators must make\, the pleasures of finding new solut
 ions to old problems\, and the inevitable frustrations of never fully capt
 uring what lives in the original text.\nAcross chapters ranging from the p
 olitics of Helen of Troy to the obscenities of Aristophanes\, from Roman i
 mitation of Greek models to contemporary debates over "foreignizing" and "
 domesticizing" styles\, Wilson examines how modern norms of gender\, sexua
 lity\, violence\, humor\, and power complicate our readings of ancient wor
 ks—and how translation always risks denaturing what is unique and strang
 e about the ancient world.\nBrilliant\, erudite\, and accessible to reader
 s with no classical background\, Crossing the Wine-Dark Sea is at once an 
 introduction to the wonders of Greek and Roman literature\, a manifesto fo
 r the value of translation in a rapidly changing world\, and an invitation
  to encounter ancient cultures anew.\nEmily Wilson speaks with Ella Street
  about Crossing the Wine-Dark Sea and the lifelong work of carrying ancien
 t voices into the present.\nQ&A and book signing to follow. Books availabl
 e for purchase.\nAbout this event’s guests:\nEmily Wilson\nElla Street\n
 Read more:\nCrossing the Wine Dark-Sea : Journeys Through Ancient Literatu
 re\nThe Odyssey\nThe Iliad\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBram and Blu
 ma Appel Salon\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor\
 nTime:\nDoors open 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registrat
 ion required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to ensure that the
  greatest number of people have an opportunity to attend. Tickets guarante
 ed until 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Seating first come\, first s
 erved.\nBar\n: Beer/wine available for purchase (debit/credit only).\nAcce
 ssibility:\nTPL is committed to accessibility. If you are Deaf or have a d
 isability and would like to request accommodation for this event\, please 
 contact accessibleservices@tpl.ca or 416-393-7099 at least three weeks pri
 or to the event.\n***\nThis event is part of our signature\nSalon Series\n
 \, where we host local and international authors\, artists and thinkers in
  conversation about their new books and big ideas.\n***\nInformation Colle
 ction Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the T
 oronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20
 (c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program r
 egistration\, delivery and evaluation. Registrants may be contacted to fil
 l out a brief survey following the program. Questions about how the librar
 y handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at answerli
 ne@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Appel Salon\, Second Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b5d0bf1aec09082b9f55
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:David Treuer: The Savage Mind
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261019T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261019T200000
UID:6a85b648f564ef0d72c0eb0a@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:New York Times bestselling author David Treuer shares his grou
 ndbreaking\, intimate reckoning with the history of North America.\nThe Sa
 vage Mind is a seamless blend of memoir\, history and reportage\, in which
  Treuer leverages his unique dual perspective as the son of a Holocaust su
 rvivor and the first American Indigenous woman judge in the United States 
 to offer an examination of the distinct nature of North American violence.
 \nIn contrast to the frontier thesis of American history\, Treuer contends
  that frontier violence cannot be defined as an external force\, but that 
 it has actually entered the culture and taken root in our very selves.\nBy
  reckoning with these histories\, the book confronts our complicity in eit
 her normalizing violence\, or blaming it on outside sources\, instead of s
 eeing its recurring patterns and contending with its significance.\nDavid 
 Treuer speaks with journalist Brandi Morin about The Savage Mind and share
 s why he chooses to embrace an ultimately hopefully view of the continent.
 \nQ&A and book signing to follow. Books available for purchase.\nAbout thi
 s event’s guests:\nDavid Treuer\nBrandi Morin\nRead more:\nSavage Mind :
  An Indigenous Legacy\nThe Heartbeat of Wounded Knee\nOur Voice of Fire\n*
 **\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto Refe
 rence Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor\nTime:\nDoors open 6:00pm\, E
 vent starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registration required.\nTicket policy:
 \nWe oversell these events to ensure that the greatest number of people ha
 ve an opportunity to attend. Tickets guaranteed until 6:45pm or until capa
 city is reached. Seating first come\, first served.\nBar:\nBeer/wine avail
 able for purchase (debit/credit only).\n***\nThis event is part of our sig
 nature\nSalon Series\n\, where we host local and international authors\, a
 rtists and thinkers in conversation about their new books and big ideas.\n
 It is also part of our\nIndigenous Heritage series\n\, generously supporte
 d by TD Bank Group\, through the TD Ready Commitment.\n***\nInformation Co
 llection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by th
 e Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\,
  20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and progra
 m registration\, delivery and evaluation. Registrants may be contacted to 
 fill out a brief survey following the program. Questions about how the lib
 rary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at answe
 rline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Appel Salon\, Second Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b648f564ef0d72c0eb0a
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
