BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Dufferin/St. Clair Book Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261006T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261006T200000
UID:6958356a1e64afd01e520491@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us the first Tuesday of each month to discuss books in a 
 fun and friendly atmosphere.\nCopies are available for pickup at the Duffe
 rin/St. Clair branch each month\, or place your own hold to have a copy se
 nt to your closest branch.\nAugust:\nThe Covenant of Water\nby Abraham Ve
 rghese\nSeptember:\nThe Corrections\nby Jonathan Frazen\nOctober:\nThe Edi
 ble Woman\nby Margaret Atwood\nNovember:\nSlammerkin\nby Emma Donoghue\nD
 ecember:\nWhat We Can Know\nby Ian McEwan
LOCATION:Dufferin/St.Clair\, Earlscourt Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6958356a1e64afd01e520491
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Monthly Movie Screening: Past Lives
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260917T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260917T200000
UID:6a68e5507b79214226abe9d8@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:This month's feature:\nPast Lives\nDescription: Nora and Hae 
 Sung\, two deeply connected childhood friends\,                           
        are wrest apart after Nora's family emigrates from South Korea. Two
  decades later\,                                      they are reunited in
  New York for one fateful week as they confront notions of                
                               destiny\, love\, and the choices that make a
  life\, in this heartrending modern                                       
    romance.\nThis film is rated PG-13. 106 minutes. Drop in. All are welco
 me!
LOCATION:Palmerston\, Downstairs theatre
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a68e5507b79214226abe9d8
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Dystopia Nonfiction Book Club: The Crisis of Canadian Democracy
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260914T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260914T153000
UID:6a5f72a5f4e5db3d00c32d57@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us each month to discuss issues in politics and society\,
  and explore how powerful forces are exploiting our world and dystopian im
 aginings are becoming present-day realities.\nSeptember’s book is\nThe C
 risis of Canadian Democracy\nby journalist Andrew Coyne. Copies of the bo
 ok are available in print and eBook format. Book club attendees are encou
 raged to read the book in advance of the meeting.\nThe Crisis of Canadian
  Democracy "dismantles the comforting myths Canadians tell themselves abou
 t their political system\, revealing a parliamentary structure eroded by u
 naccountable leaders\, disempowered MPs\, manipulated elections\, and syst
 emic dysfunction. [The book] is both a wake-up call and a call to action\
 , offering compelling solutions to restore genuine self-government to Cana
 dian politics." Andrew Coyne warns Canadians that "as Parliament's legitim
 acy declines\, people look to alternate sources of representation... In ti
 me\, as we have seen south of the border\, the discrediting of democratic 
 institutions can begin to nourish a desire for autocracy."\nDrop in. No re
 gistration required.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Lower Level\, Room B1
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a5f72a5f4e5db3d00c32d57
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:English Conversation Circle
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261117T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261117T150000
UID:6a5bcff70ed7b4521ed65fb1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Are you looking to practice your English speaking skills? Come
  out to English Conversation Circle! Meet likeminded peers and practice yo
 ur conversation in a supportive\, casual environment. Please note that thi
 s is not a formal English class.
LOCATION:Lillian H. Smith\, Room B/C
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a5bcff70ed7b4521ed65fb1
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:NatureTO: Trees Around the Branch Tour
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261007T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261007T123000
UID:6a6cc7a274e3b8b75db66fdd@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:The Ethennonnhawahstihnen’ branch and its neighbourhood is h
 ome to a variety of trees. Join the\nCity of Toronto Urban Forestry\nteam\
 nto explore the natural world on a local neighbourhood tour. Learn how to 
 recognize different species and fun facts about their cultural and ecologi
 cal importance.\nFree program. Children under 12 must be accompanied by a 
 parent/caregiver.\nPresented as part of TPL’s environmental program seri
 es\, Our Fragile Planet. The series is generously supported by Friends of 
 Toronto Public Library\, South Chapter.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nT
 he personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Li
 brary under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d).
  The information will only be used for event and program registration\, de
 livery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your inform
 ation can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-39
 7-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8
LOCATION:Ethennonnhawahstihnen'
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a6cc7a274e3b8b75db66fdd
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Knitter Natter
