BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:We Need To Talk About AI: AI for the Non-Technical
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261007T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261007T200000
UID:6a5912f30e562e28009a4ea9@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Curious about Artificial Intelligence (AI)?\nJoin us to learn 
 about and discuss aspects of this technological innovation.\nThis workshop
  series explores AI's real impacts on jobs\, privacy\, the environment\, a
 nd our communities\, while discussing how we can shape it responsibly. Eac
 h themed 90-minute session allows for small group discussion\, practical t
 akeaways and honest conversation about AI without the hype.\nOct 7 - AI f
 or the Non-Technical\nIn this session\, we tackle the\n"Elephant in the Ro
 om"\n—addressing ethics\, hallucinations\, and the fear of replacement t
 o foster a responsible\n"AI for Good"\nmindset. We demystify the technolog
 y by viewing AI as an\n"intern with a PhD in everything"\n—a tool that m
 asters five specific superpowers\, such as personalization at scale and ra
 pid research\, while leaving the irreplaceable domain of empathy\, intuiti
 on\, and creativity to you.\nOct 14 - How Do We Shape AI?\nAI is already 
 reshaping how we work\, protect our privacy\, and participate in society
 —yet most of us never get a seat at the table in deciding how it's built
  or used. This session creates that space. Equal parts presentation and co
 nversation\,
LOCATION:Lillian H. Smith\, Room B/C
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a5912f30e562e28009a4ea9
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SP Book Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261022T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261022T150000
UID:698bcbf87d180d0d4833345c@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Discuss diverse and inclusive titles from a variety of genres 
 on the fourth Thursday of every month!\nThis month we will be discussing\n
 Rouge\nby Mona Awad.\nWhen Belle returns to La Jolla for her beauty-obsess
 ed mother’s funeral\, she is lured into a surreal\, cult-like world cent
 ered around a mysterious spa and its glowing red jellyfish. This descent i
 nto "Sephora Horror" blends grief with gory mayhem to expose the suffocati
 ng toxicity of the beauty industry. Ultimately\, it is a ferocious fairy t
 ale about the dangerous obsession with eternal youth.\nPick up a physical 
 copy at the branch before the meeting\, or find copies through our website
  in various formats. Registration is required and begins Aug. 27\, 2026.\n
 Information Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is c
 ollected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, se
 ctions 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for ev
 ent and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about ho
 w the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff
  at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4
 W 2G8.
LOCATION:Spadina Road\, Canoe Table\, center of branch
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/698bcbf87d180d0d4833345c
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Dystopia Nonfiction Book Club: A Good War
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261019T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261019T153000
UID:6a5f7868f4e5db3d00c32e07@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us each month to discuss issues in politics and society\,
  and explore how powerful forces are exploiting our world and dystopian im
 aginings are becoming present-day realities.\nOctober’s book is\nA Good 
 War: Mobilizing Canada for the Climate Emergency\nby public policy advisor
  Seth Klein. Copies of the book are available in print\, eBook & eAudiob
 ook format. Book club attendees are encouraged to read the book in advan
 ce of the meeting.\n"In A Good War\, Seth Klein explores how we can align 
 our politics and economy with what the science says we must do to address 
 the climate crisis. But Klein brings an original and uniquely hopeful take
  to this challenge. The book is structured around lessons from the Second 
 World War – the last time Canada faced an existential threat. Others hav
 e said we need a “wartime approach” to climate change\, but this is th
 e first book to delve into what that could actually look like. Canada’s 
 wartime experience\, Klein contends\, provides an inspirational reminder t
 hat\nwe have done this before\n. We have mobilized in common cause across 
 class\, race and gender\, and entirely retooled our economy in the space o
 f a few short years."\nDrop in. No registration required.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Lower Level\, Room B1
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a5f7868f4e5db3d00c32e07
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
