BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Ghost Stories of Toronto: Colonial Spectres
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260918T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260918T130000
UID:6a85b74f4c610f0038954f4a@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:"It’s only by our lack of ghosts we’re haunted\," the poet
  Earle Birney once said of Canada. But the truth is that people have been 
 telling supernatural tales in Toronto since the city was founded\, and lon
 g before that\, too. We'll begin this series of talks with author Adam Bun
 ch by heading back to the city's founding days as a settlement to explore 
 ghost stories from the early 1800s and see what they have to reveal about 
 colonialism\, the War of 1812\, and the fears of the settlers who lived in
  this place two hundred years ago.\nAbout this event's guest:\nAdam Bunch\
 nRead More:\nThe Toronto Book of Love\nThe Toronto Book of the Dead\nAbout
  this series:\nYou can learn a lot about Toronto through its ghost stories
 . Whether you believe in the supernatural or not\, the tales we tell about
  spirits and spectres illuminate the true history of the city. They're oft
 en tied to dramatic stories of death and disaster\, to fascinating histori
 c sites\, and to pivotal moments that helped shape our communities. In thi
 s series of talks by author Adam Bunch\, we'll explore Toronto's past thro
 ugh tales of ghosts\, séances and hauntings. Whether it's phantom horses 
 from the War of 1812\, the strange prime minister who believed in the occu
 lt\, or the ghost who was arrested by the Toronto Police Morality Squad\, 
 these supernatural stories have a lot to teach us about the history of the
  place we call home.\n***\nImportant information for accessing this live v
 irtual event:\nYou must register for a free ticket via Eventbrite to gain 
 access to this event.\nThis event will be presented in a Zoom Webinar. Onc
 e registered\, you will receive reminder emails from both Zoom and Eventbr
 ite with links and instructions to access your unique Zoom Webinar link to
  watch this event.\nAt the time of the event\, you must be signed in to Zo
 om or Eventbrite with the same email address that you used to book your ti
 cket in order to access your unique event link. Unique event links can onl
 y be open on one device at a time.\n***\nAccessibility at Toronto Public L
 ibrary\nWe are committed to making our content accessible to as many Toron
 tonians as possible. Please call or email us if you are Deaf or have a dis
 ability and would like to request accommodation to participate in this pro
 gram. Please let us know as far in advance as possible and we will do our 
 best to meet your request. At least three weeks' notice is preferred. Phon
 e 416-393-7099 or email accessibleservices@tpl.ca\n***\nInformation Collec
 tion Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the To
 ronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(
 c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program re
 gistration\, delivery and evaluation.\nRegistrants may be contacted to fil
 l out a brief survey following the program\n. Questions about how the libr
 ary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at answer
 line@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b74f4c610f0038954f4a
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Ghost Stories of Toronto: Victorian Spirits
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261009T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261009T130000
UID:6a85b74e2f41840bbfc3077e@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:The Victorian age is famously associated with ghost stories\, 
 and Victorian Toronto is certainly no exception. For the second talk in th
 is series\, author Adam Bunch will explore some of our city's spookiest su
 pernatural tales from the late 1800s\, an era that provides us with storie
 s of haunted mansions\, secret societies\, and the spirits said to roam th
 e halls of some of our finest public buildings.\nAbout this event's guest:
 \nAdam Bunch\nRead More:\nThe Toronto Book of Love\nThe Toronto Book of th
 e Dead\nAbout this series:\nYou can learn a lot about Toronto through its 
 ghost stories. Whether you believe in the supernatural or not\, the tales 
 we tell about spirits and spectres illuminate the true history of the city
 . They're often tied to dramatic stories of death and disaster\, to fascin
 ating historic sites\, and to pivotal moments that helped shape our commun
 ities. In this series of talks by author Adam Bunch\, we'll explore Toront
 o's past through tales of ghosts\, séances and hauntings. Whether it's ph
 antom horses from the War of 1812\, the strange prime minister who believe
 d in the occult\, or the ghost who was arrested by the Toronto Police Mora
 lity Squad\, these supernatural stories have a lot to teach us about the h
 istory of the place we call home.\n***\nImportant information for accessin
 g this live virtual event:\nYou must register for a free ticket via Eventb
 rite to gain access to this event.\nThis event will be presented in a Zoom
  Webinar. Once registered\, you will receive reminder emails from both Zoo
 m and Eventbrite with links and instructions to access your unique Zoom We
 binar link to watch this event.\nAt the time of the event\, you must be si
 gned in to Zoom or Eventbrite with the same email address that you used to
  book your ticket in order to access your unique event link. Unique event 
 links can only be open on one device at a time.\n***\nAccessibility at Tor
 onto Public Library\nWe are committed to making our content accessible to 
 as many Torontonians as possible. Please call or email us if you are Deaf 
 or have a disability and would like to request accommodation to participat
 e in this program. Please let us know as far in advance as possible and we
  will do our best to meet your request. At least three weeks' notice is pr
 eferred. Phone 416-393-7099 or email accessibleservices@tpl.ca\n***\nInfor
 mation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collec
 ted by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, section
 s 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event a
 nd program registration\, delivery and evaluation.\nRegistrants may be con
 tacted to fill out a brief survey following the program\n. Questions about
  how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline st
 aff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4
 W 2G8.
