BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Hank Idsinga: The High Road
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261109T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261109T200000
UID:6a85b934f564ef0d72c0eb91@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:TPL presents retired homicide detective and author Hank Idsing
 a in conversation with Wendy Gillis at the North York Central Library's Co
 ncourse Event Space.\nIn his bestselling memoir\nThe High Road: Confession
 s of a Homicide Cop\n\, Idsinga takes readers behind the crime scene tape 
 and into the mean streets of Toronto's most notorious murders. Teaming up 
 with partners including future police chief Mark Saunders\, Idsinga built 
 a reputation for excellent recall and sharp instinct\, finding motive and 
 method faster than most. As the public face of that investigation\, Idsing
 a remained strong and compassionate before the cameras\, even as the polic
 e force faced fierce criticism over its handling of the case.\nHow do homi
 cide detectives carry the weight of the cases they never solve? What does 
 real accountability within policing actually look like\, and why does it m
 atter? And what can three decades on the front lines of Toronto's worst cr
 imes teach us about justice\, institutional trust\, and the human cost of 
 violence?\nJoin veteran crime reporter Wendy Gillis in conversation with H
 ank Idsinga for a gripping\, candid evening inside the world of Canada's m
 ost storied homicide unit.\nQ&A and book signing to follow. Books availabl
 e for purchase.\nAbout this event’s guests:\nHank Idsinga\nWendy Gillis\
 nRead more:\nThe High Road\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nConcourse Ev
 ent Space\, North York Central Library (5120 Yonge St)\, Lower Level\nTime
 :\nDoors open 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registration r
 equired.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to ensure that the grea
 test number of people have an opportunity to attend. Tickets guaranteed un
 til 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Seating first come\, first served
 .\nAccessibility:\nTPL is committed to accessibility. If you are Deaf or h
 ave a disability and would like to request accommodation for this event\, 
 please contact accessibleservices@tpl.ca or 416-393-7099 at least three we
 eks prior to the event.\n***\nThis event is part of our signature\nOn Civi
 l Society\nseries\, where we host local and international authors\, activi
 sts and academics in conversation about the most critical topics of our ti
 me. Generously supported by the McConnell Foundation and also by Chris M. 
 Reid.\n***\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on th
 is form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Librar
 ies Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be
  used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Regist
 rants may be contacted to fill out a brief survey following the program. Q
 uestions about how the library handles your information can be directed to
  Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\,
  Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Concourse Event Space\, North York C
 entral Library (5120 Yonge St)\, Lower Level
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b934f564ef0d72c0eb91
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Jill Lepore: The Artificial State
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261001T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261001T200000
UID:6a85b8d14c610f0038954fe6@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:TPL and Toronto Metropolitan University present Pulitzer-Prize
  winning historian Jill Lepore in conversation with Sanjay Ruparelia at th
 e Toronto Reference Library's Appel Salon.\nAs Professor of American Histo
 ry at Harvard University and Professor of Law at Harvard Law School\, Jill
  Lepore brings her rare gift for narrative history to one of the most urge
 nt questions of our time: has democracy already been quietly replaced by r
 ule of a different kind\, one governed not by citizens\, but by machines o
 wned by corporations?\nIn her new book\nThe Rise and Fall of the Artificia
 l State\n\, Lepore traces our increasing dependence on and strangulation b
 y data. Framing the book as a successor to Hannah Arendt's\nThe Origins of
  Totalitarianism\n\, Lepore shares a new history for our twenty-first cent
 ury.\nWhat does it mean when the era of the liberal nation-state seems to 
 be giving way to the rule of artificial intelligence? Lepore insists histo
 ry is driven not by machines but by individuals\, societies\, cultures\, a
 nd ideas\, and that the architects of the Artificial State drew their gove
 rning philosophy not from science\, but from science fiction.\nCan what ha
 s been built be undone? And what would it take to reimagine a genuinely de
 mocratic relationship between technology and self-governance\, one where h
 umans\, not machines\, remain accountable to one another?\nJoin one of Ame
 rica's most celebrated historians\, fresh from her bestselling\nWe the Peo
 ple: A History of the U.S. Constitution\n\, for an essential conversation 
 about power\, technology\, and the fight to reclaim democratic life from t
 he architects of an artificial future.\nQuestion and answer session and bo
 ok signing to follow. Books available for purchase.\nAbout this Event's Gu
 ests:\nJill Lepore\nSanjay Ruparelia\nRead More:\nRise and Fall of the Art
 ificial State\nWe the People\n***\nThis event is part of our\nOn Civil Soc
 iety\nseries\, where we host local and international authors\, activists a
 nd academics in conversation about the most critical topics of our time. G
 enerously supported by the McConnell Foundation and also by Chris M. Reid.
 \n***\nAbout the series:\nOn the Frontlines of Democracy\nis a public lect
 ure series to analyze the trajectory of democracy in the twenty-first cent
 ury.\nIn every region\, the prospects of democracy face serious challenges
 \, old and new. Can we protect constitutional rights and renew collective 
 self-rule in an era of popular mistrust\, severe partisanship and resurgen
 t nationalism? Can they reduce inequalities of power\, wealth and status\,
  defend deep diversity and confront climate change in the new digital age?
  Can we develop innovative strategies to revitalize civic engagement\, emp
 ower public institutions and resist autocratic threats? And what can we of
 fer\, learn and do to promote the prospects of democracy in our increasing
 ly post-western global order?\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT\n:\nLocation: Bram and
  Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd fl
 oor\nTime: Doors open 6:00 pm\, Event starts 7 pm.\nTickets: Free registra
 tion required.\nTicket policy: Tickets guaranteed until 6:50 pm\, after wh
 ich rush line admission begins. Seating first come\, first served.\nBar: B
 eer/wine available for purchase (debit/credit only).\nTPL is committed to 
 accessibility. If you are Deaf or have a disability and would like to requ
 est accommodation for this event\, please contact accessibleservices@tpl.c
 a or 416-393-7099 at least three weeks prior to the event.\n***\nInformati
 on Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected 
 by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20
 (a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and p
 rogram registration\, delivery and evaluation.\nRegistrants may be contact
 ed to fill out a brief survey following the program\n. Questions about how
  the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff 
 at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G
 8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Bram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto 
 Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b8d14c610f0038954fe6
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Lede: A Live Editorial Meeting Experience
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T200000
UID:6a85b8bdf564ef0d72c0eb8c@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:TPL and the Canadian Journalism Foundation present a special M
 edia Literacy week event!\nThree journalists. One front page. Only one sto
 ry leads.\nThe Lede pulls back the curtain on a process most people never 
 see: how editors weigh impact\, timeliness\, public interest and audience 
 relevance under pressure\, in real time.\nThree journalists from The Globe
  and Mail\, Canada's national newspaper\, deputy head of investigations Ro
 byn Doolittle\, politics and economy editor Ryan MacDonald and senior visu
 als editor Solana Cain\, will each pitch the story they believe deserves t
 he lead position in tomorrow's news. Editor-in-chief David Walmsley will q
 uestion their assumptions\, challenge their reasoning and make the final c
 all\, live\, in front of the audience.\nThe audience does not just watch. 
 Before Walmsley announces his decision\, they vote on which story should l
 ead\, then see how their judgment compares with his.\nJoin us for a chance
  to understand not just what the news is\, but how it gets made\, and to w
 alk away a sharper\, more critical reader of it.\nAbout this event’s gue
 sts:\nDavid Walmsley\nis\nThe Globe and Mail\n’s editor-in-chief and the
  current president of the World Editors Forum. He is the co-creator of Wor
 ld News Day\, a global day of education involving more than 1\,000 publish
 ers across 100 countries explaining the role of journalism and how it can 
 make the world a better place. Under his editorship\, The Globe has won th
 e annual Canadian Michener Award for meritorious public service journalism
  five times and has dominated the annual National Newspaper Awards.\nRobyn
  Doolittle\nis the deputy head of investigations at\nThe Globe and Mail\n.
  Doolittle came to\nThe Globe\nin 2014 to join the investigative unit. Aft
 er a decade on the i-Team\, she spent time in the Report on Business cover
 ing corporate law. She has investigated political and business corruption\
 , a series of shootings and arsons targeting Canadian waste giant GFL\, th
 e Hockey Canada scandal\, gender inequities in the workforce and Canada’
 s broken freedom of information system as part of the Secret Canada projec
 t. Her two books\, “Had It Coming – What’s Fair In The Age of #MeToo
 ?\,” and “Crazy Town: The Rob Ford Story\,” were both national bests
 ellers.\nRyan MacDonald\nis the editor of politics and economy at\nThe Glo
 be and Mail\n\, leading a talented team of reporters covering the intersec
 tion of business and economic policy\, including energy\, finance\, trade 
 and fiscal matters. Since joining\nThe Globe\nin 2007\, MacDonald has held
  multiple editorial positions and was notably part of the team that won th
 e 2022 CJF Climate Solutions Reporting Award for its narrative-shifting st
 ories on how to re-engineer Canada’s economy to adapt to and capitalize 
 on climate change. Prior to joining\nThe Globe\n\, MacDonald served as the
  national editor at the\nOttawa Citizen\n.\nSolana Cain\nis a senior visua
 ls editor at\nThe Globe and Mail\n\, Canada’s national newspaper. She jo
 ined\nThe Globe\nthrough the 2019 summer program\, quickly distinguishing 
 herself as a perceptive and composed photo editor. In 2020\, Cain helped e
 stablish Room Up Front\, a mentorship initiative supporting BIPOC photojou
 rnalists. The program received a special citation at the 2023 National New
 spaper Awards for its significant contribution to Canadian journalism. A d
 edicated mentor and educator\, she actively advises emerging photographers
  and lectures at Toronto Metropolitan University\, Sheridan College\, and 
 Loyalist College.\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBram and Bluma Appel 
 Salon\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor\nTime:\nD
 oors open 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registration requi
 red.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to ensure that the greatest
  number of people have an opportunity to attend. Tickets guaranteed until 
 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Seating first come\, first served.\nB
 ar\n: Beer/wine available for purchase (debit/credit only).\nAccessibility
 :\nTPL is committed to accessibility. If you are Deaf or have a disability
  and would like to request accommodation for this event\, please contact a
 ccessibleservices@tpl.ca or 416-393-7099 at least three weeks prior to the
  event.\n***\nThis event is part of our signature\nOn Civil Society\nserie
 s\, where we host local and international authors\, activists and academic
 s in conversation about the most critical topics of our time. Generously s
 upported by the McConnell Foundation and also by Chris M. Reid.\n***\nInfo
 rmation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is colle
 cted by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sectio
 ns 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event 
 and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Registrants may be con
 tacted to fill out a brief survey following the program. Questions about h
 ow the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staf
 f at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 
 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Bram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto 
 Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor
