BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Justin Gray:  Building a Life in Music: Observations from the Stud
 io\, Stage\, and Classroom
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260905T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260905T160000
UID:6a6a2e8be9de6536001f10c6@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:T.U. Jazz Fest 2026 Masterclass Series\nJustin Gray is a 2026 
 Grammy award-wining producer and a Juno award-winning bassist\, composer\,
  engineer\, producer\, and educator based in Toronto\, ON. As a double bas
 sist\, electric bassist\, and Bass Veena player\, Justin performs in a var
 iety of traditional and contemporary music ensembles from around the world
 . Justin is on faculty at Humber College in Toronto\, where he teaches au
 dio production\, performance\, and composition.  As a producer\, mix engin
 eer and mastering engineer\, Justin has worked with artists from around th
 e world in a wide range of musical styles.\nOver his career\, Justin has r
 eceived several awards and distinctions\, including 2020 CFMA Nominee (Bes
 t World Album)\, 2018 CFMA Nominee (Best Solo Artist)\, 2017 Canadian Scre
 en Award (Co-Producer of Best Original Song)\, 2015 Emerging Jazz Artist A
 ward (Toronto Arts Foundation) and 2015 CFMA Nomination (Best World Album)
 .\nNo advance registration required. All ages welcome. For more informatio
 n\, please call the Languages\, Literature & Fine Arts Department at 416-
 395-5639.\nIn partnership with the Emerging Artists Association
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Auditorium\,  2nd floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a6a2e8be9de6536001f10c6
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Paper Trail: Writing and Walking by Sarah Ellis
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261008T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261008T193000
UID:6a7f616a4cb69d003e20d6fd@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Photo credit: Thelonius Mthombeni\nJoin award-winning author\,
  teacher and librarian Sarah Ellis for the upcoming 38th Annual Helen E. S
 tubbs Memorial Lecture at our Osborne Collection of Early Children’s Boo
 ks.\nThe word that best describes Sarah's career in writing is "meander." 
 In this presentation she will tell stories about her work and her wanders\
 , including the pleasures of flaneuring\, both on the page and on her feet
 \, the winding journey from grade four essay to substack via a long detour
  in children's fiction\, her beloved companions of the road\, the importan
 ce of good socks and good colleagues\, the inevitable\, scary and producti
 ve experience of getting lost\, and the odd tendency of humans to walk cou
 nterclockwise when left to wander freely (true fact!). Come prepared for d
 etours.\nSarah Ellis is the award-winning author of over twenty-five books
  for children and young adults. In 2013 she was awarded the B.C. Lieutenan
 t Governor’s Award for Literary Excellence. She is a three-time Canadian
  nominee for the prestigious Astrid Lindgren Memorial Award. Having retire
 d as a children’s librarian and then retired from college teaching she i
 s now writing and reading fulltime in the rain in Vancouver\, sharing her 
 bookish thoughts online at "Wild Reading" on substack. Her latest book for
  children is As Glenn as Can Be illustrated by Nancy Vo\, published by Gro
 undwood Books and she has a recent adult memoir\, My Year in Fairyland: Gr
 ief\, MAID and a Lifetime of Reading published by Stonehewer Books.
LOCATION:Lillian H. Smith\, Lower Level-Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a7f616a4cb69d003e20d6fd
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Coffee and Conversation
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T093000
