BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Postcards of Reconnection: A Craft Workshop
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261005T173000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261005T183000
UID:6a95c15536204e08fbb889a8@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Postcards of Reconnection\nSometimes reconnecting starts in so
 litude\, not small talk. In this quiet\, reflective craft workshop\, you
 ’ll design and illustrate a postcard for someone you've been meaning to 
 reach: a friend\, a family member\, or anyone lingering in your thoughts. 
 Take it home\, add a stamp when you're ready and pop it into the mail\, ta
 king the first step towards an important reconnection.\nThis interactive p
 re-show is followed by\nFrom Solitude To Connetion ft. Robert Coplan\,\nwh
 ich starts at 7pm in the North York Central Library Concourse.\nYou can re
 gister for that event\nhere\n\, but please note if you wish to attend the 
 postcard workshop you need to register separately\non this page\n.\n***\nS
 pend an evening rethinking time alone in a room full of other people\n\, a
 s\nwe exp\nlore the\ntheme\nof\n“good solitude” and how alone time mak
 es togetherness possible\n.\nMake the most of your\nConnect IRL\nexperienc
 e by coming before or staying after the\nfeatured event\n. Grab a drink or
  snack at the bar. Sign up for the workshop or try out one of the engageme
 nt activities available\, including the Conversation Station: The Calming 
 Corner\, where you can take a moment or two to connect with a stranger aro
 und the topic of solitude.\nThe\nC\nalming\nCorner\n| 5:30 – 6:30 pm & 8
 :00 – 9:00 pm\nThe Calming Corner offers a calmer\, low-sensory space wi
 th small seating pods of two or three\, prompt cards on solitude and recha
 rge habits\, and no pressure to mingle beyond your comfort zone. It's buil
 t on Coplan's own research that meaningful connection doesn't require cons
 tant stimulation\, just the right amount of space to think before you spea
 k.\nConnect IRL\nis not your average series of events\, workshops and acti
 vities\, but a real chance to connect with other Torontonians in meaningfu
 l and sometimes unexpected ways. Through conversation stations meant to sp
 ark one-on-one conversations\, or hands-on engagement activities and works
 hops before the featured event\, participants can explore the value of con
 nection and community.\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nConcourse Event 
 Space\, North York Central Library (5120 Yonge St).\nTime:\nDoors open 5:3
 0pm.\nTickets:\nFree registration required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell t
 hese events to ensure that the greatest number of people have an opportuni
 ty to attend. Tickets guaranteed until 5:45pm or until capacity is reached
 . Seating first come\, first served.\nBar\n: Beer/wine available for purch
 ase (debit/credit only).\nAccessibility:\nTPL is committed to accessibilit
 y. If you are Deaf or have a disability and would like to request accommod
 ation for this event\, please contact accessibleservices@tpl.ca or 416-393
 -7099 at least three weeks prior to the event.\n***\nInformation Collectio
 n Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toron
 to Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\
 , and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program regis
 tration\, delivery and evaluation. Registrants may be contacted to fill ou
 t a brief survey following the program. Questions about how the library ha
 ndles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@t
 pl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Concourse Event Space\, North York C
 entral Library (5120 Yonge St).
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a95c15536204e08fbb889a8
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:From Solitude To Connetion ft. Robert Coplan
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261005T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261005T200000
UID:6a95c20260ccaf01c0225378@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Spend an evening rethinking time alone in a room full of other
  people as we explore the theme of “good solitude” and how alone time 
 makes togetherness possible.\nMake the most of your\nConnect IRL\nexperien
 ce by coming before or staying after the featured event. Grab a drink or s
 nack at the bar.\nSign up for the Postcards of Re-Connections Workshop\n(s
 ee below for registration) or try out one of the engagement activities ava
 ilable\, including the\nConversation\nStation:\nThe\nC\nalming\nCorner\n\,
  where you can take a moment or two to connect with a stranger around the 
 topic of solitude.\n****\nJoin us at the North York Central Library Concou
 rse for\nBeing Alone Together\nwith psychologist and author Robert Coplan\
 nin conversation with Alanna Mitchell\n.\nTogether\, we’ll take apart th
 e assumption that solitude and connection pull in opposite directions\, ex
 amine the difference between chosen alone time and painful disconnection\,
  and talk about how much of each we actually need. Expect to leave with a 
 clearer sense of your own limits\, and a few practical ways to protect the
  quiet that makes company possible.\nWhat does "good" solitude actually lo
 ok like\, and how is it different from the isolation so many people are st
 ruggling with? How do extraverts and introverts experience alone time diff
 erently\, and what do we get wrong about both? And in a culture that treat
 s constant availability as a virtue\, what would it mean to reclaim solitu
 de as a skill worth practicing — not an escape from community\, but a fo
 undation for building it?\nCoplan\, a Carleton University psychologist and
  one of the world's leading researchers on solitude\, draws on psychology\
 , neuroscience\, literature and sociology in The Joy of Solitude: How to R
 econnect with Yourself in an Overconnected World. He distinguishes lonelin
 ess from solitude\, introduces "aloneliness" — the real distress of not 
 having enough time to yourself — and shows that even fifteen minutes alo
 ne a day can stabilize mood\, spark creativity and recharge our capacity f
 or connection.\nCome for a thoughtful conversation about the surprising ro
 le alone time plays in helping us feel closer to others.\nQ&A to follow. B
 ooks available for purchase.\nAbout this event’s guests:\nRobert Coplan\
 nAlanna Mitchell\nRead more:\nThe Joy of Solitude\n***\nSpend an evening r
 ethinking time alone in a room full of other people\n\, as\nwe exp\nlore t
 he\ntheme\nof\n“good solitude” and how alone time makes togetherness p
 ossible\n.\nMake the most of your\nConnect IRL\nexperience by coming befor
 e or staying after the featured event. Grab a drink or snack at the bar.\n
 Sign up for the workshop\nor try out one of the engagement activities avai
 lable\, including the\nConversation\nStation:\nThe\nC\nalming\nCorner\n\, 
 where you can take a moment or two to connect with a stranger around the t
 opic of solitude.\nThe Calming Corner\n| 5:30 – 6:30 pm & 8:00 – 9:00 
 pm\nThe Calming Corner offers a calmer\, low-sensory space with small seat
 ing pods of two or three\, prompt cards on solitude and recharge habits\, 
 and no pressure to mingle beyond your comfort zone. It's built on Coplan's
  own research that meaningful connection doesn't require constant stimulat
 ion\, just the right amount of space to think before you speak.\nConnect I
 RL\nis not your average series of events\, workshops and activities\, but 
 a real chance to connect with other Torontonians in meaningful and sometim
 es unexpected ways. Through conversation stations meant to spark one-on-on
 e conversations\, or hands-on engagement activities and workshops before t
 he featured event\, participants can explore the value of connection and c
 ommunity.\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nConcourse Event Space\, North
  York Central Library (5120 Yonge St).\nTime:\nDoors open 5:30pm\, Onstage
  event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registration required.\nTicket polic
 y:\nWe oversell these events to ensure that the greatest number of people 
 have an opportunity to attend. Tickets guaranteed until 6:45pm or until ca
 pacity is reached. Seating first come\, first served.\nBar\n: Beer/wine av
 ailable for purchase (debit/credit only).\nAccessibility:\nTPL is committe
 d to accessibility. If you are Deaf or have a disability and would like to
  request accommodation for this event\, please contact accessibleservices@
 tpl.ca or 416-393-7099 at least three weeks prior to the event.\n***\nInfo
 rmation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is colle
 cted by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sectio
 ns 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event 
 and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Registrants may be con
 tacted to fill out a brief survey following the program. Questions about h
 ow the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staf
 f at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 
 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Concourse Event Space\, North York C
 entral Library (5120 Yonge St)\, Lower Level
